'''Atsui Mas''' ({{lang|ja|もっと熱い}}, "hotter") is a casual Sanese counter-service restaurant in the [[Carrer del Canal]] neighborhood of [[Sant Cristòfor]], [[Balboa]]. A sibling concept to the fine-dining restaurant [[Atsui (restaurant)|Atsui]], it was opened in 2017 by brothers [[Franz Méliz]] and [[Martin Méliz]] to serve Sanese rice bowls at budget prices during breakfast and lunch. Bowls are priced between 4 and 8 [[International Dollar|$]]. The restaurant operates on a counter-service model with no reservations, and is known for the long queues that form daily before its opening.

== Name ==

The name is a play on the Sanese word ''atsui'' (熱い, "hot"), with the addition of ''mas'' ({{lang|es|más}}, "more" in Volisanian), yielding the bilingual pun "hotter" or "more hot." The name signals both the restaurant's relationship to [[Atsui (restaurant)|Atsui]] and its more accessible, casual character — a hotter kitchen turning out faster food.

== History ==

Following [[Atsui (restaurant)|Atsui]]'s rise to international prominence — culminating in the Nº1 position on [[Abyala's 50 Best Restaurants]] in 2015 — the Méliz brothers faced a practical dilemma. The omakase format at Atsui served fourteen guests per evening at a premium price point, leaving the flavors of Sanese cuisine largely inaccessible to the Sant Cristòfor residents who had supported the restaurant in its early years. Franz Méliz, who managed front-of-house operations at Atsui, had long fielded requests from locals for something more approachable.

In 2016, the brothers identified a vacant storefront at 393 [[Carrer del Canal]], in the canal-side neighborhood that had become the city's creative and dining district. The space was modest — a narrow service counter, a small open kitchen, and limited standing room — but suited the fast-turnover model they envisioned. Martin Méliz developed a menu of rice bowls based on the same Sanese flavor profiles served at Atsui, simplified for rapid execution and priced between 4 and 8 ID for a weekday clientele. The restaurant opened in early 2017.

== Cuisine ==

Atsui Mas serves Sanese rice bowls and side dishes in a counter-service format during breakfast and lunch hours. The menu is designed by [[Martin Méliz]], who maintains oversight of the kitchen's flavor profiles, while day-to-day execution is handled by a head chef deputed from the Atsui team.

=== Breakfast ===

The breakfast service, available from opening until approximately 10:30, offers a hybrid Balboan-Sanese menu. Bowls combine Sanese rice with locally sourced Balboan ingredients: scrambled eggs with Balboan chili oil and scallions over rice; grilled plantain and salted fish with miso; and a breakfast-style {{lang|ja|赤丼}} (''akadon'', "red bowl") of rice topped with leftover braised pork from the previous day's preparation, a soft-fried egg, and a drizzle of koregusu soy. The breakfast menu is the most distinctly Balboan of the restaurant's offerings, reflecting the morning eating habits of its local clientele.

[[File:Atsui Mas breakfast akadon.png|thumb|The breakfast ''akadon'' with braised pork and fried egg]]

=== Lunch ===

The lunch menu rotates among a core repertoire of Sanese rice bowls. Staple offerings include a {{lang|ja|辛肉飯}} (''kararōhan'', spicy braised pork rice) — diced pork belly braised in soy, five-spice, and chopped Balboan chilies, served over a large portion of unseasoned rice; a {{lang|ja|麻婆丼}} (''mapodon''), a rice bowl version of the agedashi mapo with Balboan coastal shrimp; and a grilled fish bowl featuring the day's catch from the [[Shendan Ocean|Shendan]] or [[Aethiopian Ocean|Aethiopian]] coast, cured briefly in chili salt and served with pickled vegetables and rice. Side dishes include miso soup, Balboan-style pickled papaya, and small plates of seasonal vegetables.

[[File:Atsui Mas kararōhan.png|thumb|The ''kararōhan'' (spicy braised pork rice), the most popular lunch bowl]]

As at Atsui, rice, soy sauce, miso, and koregusu are imported from Okaiken. All other ingredients are sourced locally.

=== Service model ===

The restaurant operates on a ticket-machine model common to Sanese casual dining. Upon reaching the front of the queue, customers purchase a meal ticket from a vending machine beside the entrance, selecting their bowl from illuminated buttons. The ticket is then placed on the counter, where the kitchen team retrieves it and prepares the bowl in sequence. Customers receive their bowls at the counter and eat either at a standing ledge along the window or take their meal to go. There is no table service and no reservations are accepted. The kitchen turns over bowls rapidly, with most lunch service customers completing their meal within fifteen minutes.

=== Atsui Fred ===

After the lunch service concludes, the same space reopens in the afternoon as '''Atsui Fred''' ({{lang|ja|熱い}}, "hot" + {{lang|ca|fred}}, "cold"), a Sanese-style coffee bar operating from mid-afternoon until late night. The name continues the bilingual pun convention of the Méliz brothers' branding: where Atsui Mas paired Sanese "hot" with Volisanian "more," Atsui Fred pairs it with the Volisanian word for "cold," signaling the shift from the heat of the kitchen to the cool of the coffee bar.

Atsui Fred serves Sanese-style pour-over coffee prepared with Balboan-grown beans, alongside matcha, hojicha, and other traditional Sanese tea preparations. The coffee program draws on the same sourcing philosophy as the Méliz brothers' restaurants: Balboan ingredients prepared through Sanese technique.

In addition to drinks, Atsui Fred offers a small menu of dishes called {{lang|ca|finals especials}} ("special ends"), prepared from the waste products and tailings of the lunch service — trimmings, off-cuts, and residual braising liquids repurposed into small plates. The menu changes daily depending on what the lunch kitchen has left, and is typically written by hand on a board behind the counter each afternoon. The concept has drawn attention in local food media for its zero-waste approach and the inventiveness of its preparations.

== Queues and popularity ==

Atsui Mas is known for the long lines that form before each service. On weekdays, the queue typically extends thirty to forty-five minutes; on weekends, wait times can exceed an hour. The restaurant's popularity is attributed to its combination of Sanese flavors — otherwise available in Sant Cristòfor only at the much more expensive Atsui or at a handful of informal Sanese eateries — and its budget pricing. Local media have described it as "the line that feeds the neighborhood," and the queue itself has become a minor fixture of Carrer del Canal street life.

